The function under test is open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object. Its documented rule states: “The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text, because a model places its final answer last and marks it: a reply that quotes its previous answer and then gives a corrected one is scored on the correction.”

Reply 1 is the string '```text\n{"a":1}\n```\n{"a":2}'. Reply 2 is the string '{"a":2}'.

For Reply 1, the rule requires the answer {"a":1}, because the text contains a code fence (the ```text fence) holding an object, and that fenced object is the last one inside any fence. For Reply 2, the rule requires the answer {"a":2}, because there is no fenced object, so the last top-level object in the text is {"a":2}.

The conjecture is that the check will return the same answer for both replies, namely {"a":2}. The implementation’s fence regex only matches unlabeled or json/JSON fences, so it will not treat the ```text fence as a fence. Its fenced pool will be empty for Reply 1, and it will fall through to the top-level pool, where the last top-level object is {"a":2}. Reply 2 also yields {"a":2} from the top-level pool. Thus the check collapses the difference that the documented rule requires.

What would make me wrong is the observation that the check returns different answers for the two replies—for example, if it returns {"a":1} for Reply 1 and {"a":2} for Reply 2, or if it returns any answer for Reply 1 other than {"a":2} while returning {"a":2} for Reply 2. That would show the check does distinguish the pair as the rule requires.